From: DualMesh overlay
Subject: [PATCH] Add DualMesh return-to-launcher hook to loop()

Same hook as the Meshtastic side (see ../meshtastic-overlay/), applied to
MeshCore's companion_radio example instead.

NOTE: earlier drafts of this patch relied on a global compiler "-include
dualmesh_ota.h" build flag instead of an explicit #include here, so nothing
needed to change in this file's #include list. That was reverted (and this
patch now adds the include explicitly) because a project-wide -include gets
force-injected into every translation unit PlatformIO compiles for this
target, including plain-C and framework-internal C++ files that don't have
Arduino.h on their include path — dualmesh_ota.h itself includes
<Arduino.h> and uses C++ features (namespaces), so forcing it into a
non-Arduino translation unit fails to compile. Doing the include here,
in one file that already includes <Arduino.h> on its own first line, avoids
that entirely. variants/cardputer_adv/platformio.ini still adds `-I
variants/cardputer_adv` so this quoted include resolves.

Verified against ripplebiz/MeshCore @ main (HEAD 0679dbe as of 2026-08-30),
examples/companion_radio/main.cpp: line 1 is `#include <Arduino.h>` and
loop() starts at line 247 with `the_mesh.loop();` as its first statement.
If either hunk fails to apply because upstream has moved on: add
`#include "dualmesh_ota.h"` right after the existing `#include <Arduino.h>`
near the top of the file, and add the one `dualmesh::ReturnHook::poll();`
call as the first line of `void loop() {`.
---
--- a/examples/companion_radio/main.cpp
+++ b/examples/companion_radio/main.cpp
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include <Arduino.h>   // needed for PlatformIO
+#include "dualmesh_ota.h" // DualMesh: return-to-launcher hook
 #include <Mesh.h>
 #include "MyMesh.h"

@@ -245,6 +246,7 @@
 }

 void loop() {
+  dualmesh::ReturnHook::poll(); // DualMesh: hold BOOT 3s to return to the app picker
   the_mesh.loop();
   interface_manager.loop();
   sensors.loop();
